<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Wukari</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Wukari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Jukun kingdom of Wukari in the Benue valley, ruled by a sacred king, the Aku Uka, and heir to the older power known as Kwararafa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wukari emerged as the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jukun-people">Jukun</a> centre as the older Jukun power, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kwararafa">Kwararafa</a>, declined in the 18th century. Jukun tradition holds that the royal line moved south from earlier capitals such as Kororofa and Puje, and by about 1800 Wukari, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> river, was the seat of the paramount Jukun ruler.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aku-uka">Aku Uka</a> was a sacred king, surrounded by taboos and ritual, whose well-being was believed to govern the harvest and the rains; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jukun-people">Jukun</a> ritual kingship became a classic case in anthropology. Real administration lay with officials and the heads of related Jukun communities. The economy rested on farming, salt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> valley and regional trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 19th century Wukari was hemmed in by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/muri-emirate">Muri Emirate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> raids. It came under British control in the early 1900s within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aku-uka">Aku Uka</a> remains a traditional ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraba-state">Taraba State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1800 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.52%;width:1.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wukari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jukun traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Jukun</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wukari, Jukun of Wukari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=kingdom-of-wukari" aria-label="Kingdom of Wukari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4951 1884 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Wukari lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5271 2140L5271 2140M5271 2140L5170 2068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5271 2140h0M5170 2068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 2140h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 2140h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wukari. <a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=kingdom-of-wukari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
